--- note
## Step one is always: reproduce the target
Before improving anything, we rebuilt pilkwang's LB-0.885 pipeline on our own machine — detector →
edge model → ILP → post-processing (pt01–pt08, pp01) — and scored it with the **official metric**
(me01). This is the honest baseline every idea must beat.

--- note
### The golden-12 = pilkwang's own held-out fold (leak-free)
**[Data — a lucky, important find]** pilkwang shipped his predictions for **12 datasets** (6 `44b6`
+ 6 `6bba`). Those are exactly his `split_0` **test fold** — the datasets his weights were *not*
trained on. So scoring on those 12 is **leak-free**: our local number reflects true generalisation,
not memorised training data.
--- code
GOLDEN12 = {"44b6_0113de3b", "44b6_0b24845f", ...,   # pilkwang's split_0 TEST fold
            "6bba_05b6850b", ...}                     # 6 x 44b6 + 6 x 6bba, leak-free for his weights
--- shape
12 datasets<br>= his split_0 test<br>trained on the other 187

--- note
### The result — we reproduce it
**[Data]** Running his exact pipeline on the golden-12 with the official metric:

> local golden-12 = **0.870**  ↔  public LB = **0.885**

The ~0.015 gap is the usual subset-vs-full-test wobble; the pipeline is faithfully reproduced. Now
we have a trustworthy local yardstick to test every idea against **before** spending a Kaggle
submission.
--- output
| pipeline | golden-12 (official) | public LB |
|---|---|---|
| pilkwang full | **0.870** | **0.885** |
| — raw det + ILP (no post-proc) | 0.853 | — |

--- note
### The mindset (bestfitting-style)
**[Craft]** You cannot improve what you cannot measure faithfully. Reproduce the leader, verify
your metric matches, fix a leak-free CV — *then* iterate. Everything after this (`rs02`–`rs07`)
stands on this baseline.
